Japan has approximately 7,000 islands (the largest being Honshu), making Japan the seventh largest island nation in the world!
Due to Japan's collection of islands, it is referred to in geographical terms as an "archipelago."
Taiwan's origins date back to the 15th century, when passing European navigators recorded the name of this island as Ilha Formosa, meaning "Beautiful Island."
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in southwestern Taiwan, initiated changes in local agricultural production methods, and employed Chinese workers in their rice and Chinese plantations.
In 1683, the armies of the Qing Dynasty (1644–1912) seized control of the western and eastern coastal regions of Taiwan, and in 1885, Taiwan was established as a province of the Qing Dynasty.
In 1895, after its defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Shimonoseki Treaty, under which it recognized Japan's right to take over control of Taiwan, which Japan ruled until 1945.
