Many common formats (for example, the APS family of formats) are equal to or very close to this aspect ratio.
The rule of thirds, often the target of criticism, serves as the sole guiding principle for creating movement while maintaining order and structure within a photograph.
It states that the most effective placement of the principal subject is at the intersection of the lines that divide the image into three parts vertically and horizontally.
