In ancient China, there was a unique way of representing different periods; each era or dynasty that ruled China was an exclusive royal family.
Moreover, it was an unstable period characterized by fragmented provinces among various families. The most renowned era of these times was the Three Kingdoms period, which lasted for about 60 years between the Han and Jin dynasties.
In these eras, there were intense wars among many princes and nobles for the throne and crown.
In ancient Chinese history, the Three Kingdoms period is one of the bloodiest wars, in which thousands of people lost their lives striving to sit on the highest throne in the grand palace of Jian.
There are many social and political impacts here, such as the use of the metric system, a shift from absolutism towards democracy, nationalism, and the idea that the country belongs to the common people, not to any single ruler.
