Muhammad had a deep interest in matters beyond this worldly life. He often went to a cave on Mount Nur (Light), which became famous as Hira, to engage in reflection and contemplation.
They confined themselves within the cave, and the period they spent there presents a very vivid picture of Prophet Muhammad ﷺ's spiritual experiences.
This cave, located at the summit of a mountain north of Mecca, is completely isolated from the rest of the world.
In fact, finding it is not easy, even though someone knows of its existence; once you enter the cave, there is complete isolation.
Nothing was visible above except a clear, beautiful sky and many mountains surrounding it. Very little was seen or heard about this world from the cave.
The Great Pyramids of Giza is the only one of the Seven Wonders that still stands in its place today.
The Great Pyramid, built by the Egyptians in the third century BCE, is one of several large pyramids constructed in honor of deceased pharaohs.
The Giza Plateau, also known as the Giza Necropolis, located in Egypt's Valley of the Kings, includes numerous pyramids (the Great Pyramids being the largest), several small tombs, multiple temples, and Abu Simbel.
The Great Pyramid was built in honor of the Pharaoh Khufu, and many smaller pyramids, tombs, and temples were constructed in honor of Khufu's wives and members of his family.
The "up bow" mark looks like the letter "v," and the "down bow" mark resembles a dot or a square with the bottom side missing.
It means you should start from the tip and press down, and "going down" means you should start from the middle (where your hand is holding the grip) and pull down the grip.
The upper bow generally produces a soft sound, while the lower bow is stronger and more forceful.
