After the early Middle Ages came the high Middle Ages, followed by the late Middle Ages, which ended socially around 1500.
The theory of technological determinism is a term that practically encompasses a wide range of views, from those that suggest technological advancement or the inevitability of modern technology, to the strong sense that human destiny is governed by an underlying logic embodied in scientific laws and technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two common ideas: first, that technological development follows a path that is independent of cultural or political influences, and second, that technology in turn exerts effects on societies that are inherited rather than socially conditioned.
For example, someone could say that a motorcar necessarily smoothens the way for road development.
However, it is not economically viable to lay down a road network across the country for just a few vehicles, so new production methods have been introduced to reduce the cost of car ownership.
Large-scale car ownership also leads to a high rate of accidents on roads, prompting the development of new techniques in the health sector to repair damaged bodies.
In Romanticism, there is a significant element of cultural determination, drawn from writers such as Goethe, Gottlieb Fichte, and Schiller.
In the context of romanticism, geography shaped individuals, and over time, traditions and cultures associated with that geography, which were in harmony with the society's setting, proved to be more suitable than rigid legal codes.
Just as Paris is regarded today as a center of fashion, Constantinople was considered the center of fashion in medieval Europe.
It is known to have begun around 400 CE as a center of pleasure and luxury, and it remained in existence until the 1100s CE.
The main reason for the decline in its value during the 12th century was that the Crusaders returned home carrying silk and spices as gifts, which were worth more than the prices offered in Byzantine markets.
This was the time when the title of Fashion Capital shifted from Constantinople to Paris.
Gothic style flourished between the 10th and 11th centuries and lasted until the 14th century.
Clothing was initially greatly influenced by Byzantine culture in the East.
However, due to the slow communication system, the West could lag behind by 25 to 30 years.
In the late Middle Ages, Western Europe began to develop its own style. One of the most significant advancements at the time was the popularization of the practice of using buttons to fasten the clothing of Crusaders.
Subsistence farming is a type of farming carried out only to produce food sufficient to meet the needs of the farmer and his family.
Sustainable agriculture is typically simple and often organic, combining crop rotation or other relatively straightforward practices with the use of locally adapted, protected seeds to achieve maximum yield within an ecological system.
Historically, most farmers were engaged in agriculture for their livelihood, and this situation still persists in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel alienated by mainstream social standards and provide them with a sense of identity.
The subculture may be specific due to the age, ethnicity, social class, location, and/or gender of its members.
The characteristics that distinguish a subculture may be a combination of linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or other factors.
Members of subcultures often signal their membership through distinctive and symbolic styles, bodily fashion, specific mannerisms, and unique ways of speaking.
One of the most common ways used to highlight social significance is identifying, during their upbringing, a few unpleasant incidents in which children were deliberately or accidentally ignored by adults, subjected to neglect or mistreatment, and prevented from participating in social activities.
Children like this are called "feral" or "wild." Some feral children are confined by people (usually their parents); in some cases, parents distance themselves from the child because they reject the child's severe mental or physical disability.
The wild children must have endured severe abuse or trauma before being left behind or running away.
Others are believed to have been raised by animals; some are said to have been living in the forest in their own dens.
When raised entirely by non-human animals, wild children (within psychological limits) exhibit behavior nearly identical to that of the animals raising them, such as showing fear of humans or indifference toward them.
While project-based learning should make learning easier and more interesting, the guided instructional technique provides an additional level of support.
It is not a method for learning folding, but rather it helps individuals who are going through a new learning experience, such as using a new computer program or starting a new project.
Its folds can be both virtual and physical. In other words, a teacher is one kind of fold, just as the small paper figure in Microsoft Office is also a fold.
Virtual scaffolds are internally embedded in software. Their purpose is to ask questions, prepare, and explain those methods that can challenge the student in solving problems independently.
Children are placed in foster care for various reasons, including neglect, exploitation, and even extortion.
