Even he suggests that certain abilities to interpret human behavior may exist in animals such as domesticated dogs.
Research from the 20th century has revealed that there are two sets of genetic variation: one hidden and the other visible.
Mutation includes new genetic changes, and selection eliminates those mutations from the pool of expressed variations.
There have been fluctuations back and forth between separation and integration among the two groups in every generation.
For gorillas, which have a human-like digestive system, it is difficult to meet their amino acid requirements from available plant resources in the wide savannas outside.
Moreover, failure to do so may have dangerous consequences: reduced growth, malnutrition, and ultimately, death.
The leaves and seeds of plants, which are the most easily accessible resources, contain protein, but they are difficult to digest for creatures like us unless they are cooked.
On the contrary, animals' food (ants, termites, eggs) is not only easy to digest, but also contains a high amount of protein and includes all essential amino acids.
We should not be surprised if our own ancestors solved their "protein problem" in much the same way that langurs do today, across vast areas.
Waking up during your normal sleep period and then falling back asleep (within 10–60 minutes) is a purposeful act.
This task can be easily accomplished by using a relatively quiet alarm clock, which wakes you up without fully startling you.
If you find yourself re-setting your watch while asleep, it can be placed on the other side of the room, forcing you to get out of bed to turn it off.
Other options based on biorythm include drinking a sufficient amount of fluid beverages (especially water or tea) before sleeping, which causes increased urine production and thus forces anyone to get up to urinate.
