Such persistent rhythms, which are not merely responses to external persistent cues, have been described in most living organisms, including bacteria, fungi, plants, and animals.
Biological clocks are self-sustaining oscillators that will continue to maintain their free-running cycle period even in the absence of external cues.
The Hershey-Chase experiment was the most important in establishing that DNA is genetic material.
Hershey and Chase used bacteriophages, or viruses, to transplant their DNA into bacteria.
They conducted two experiments: one to produce phage DNA using radioactive phosphorus, and another to produce phage protein using radioactive sulfur.
Depending on its type, the importance of affected genetic material, and whether the affected cells are cancerous, variation can have different effects.
Only mutations occurring in germ line cells can be passed on to children, whereas mutations in any other location may lead to cell death or cancer.
