Unfortunately, studying traffic flow is very difficult because driver behavior cannot be predicted with 100% certainty.
Fortunately, drivers tend to behave within appropriate, consistent limits; thus, there is a certain degree of stability in traffic flows, which can be to some extent described in mathematical terms.
To better manage traffic flow, relationships have been established among three key factors: (1) flow, (2) density, and (3)
These connections assist in planning, design, and the implementation of highway facilities.
Insects were the first organisms to take to the air. Their ability to fly helped them eliminate enemies more easily and find food and mates more effectively.
Most insects benefit from the ability to join their wings back to the body.
It provides them with a wide range of small places to hide from wild animals.
Today, the only insects that cannot fold their wings backward are bees or wasps.
