On the other side of the image, a person transforms himself/herself into an unidentifiable individual who feels the need to completely transform and create the tasks being carried out by the team.
Ultimately, the leader is finally accountable for the team's success or failure.
This behavior often creates rifts between leaders and the rest of the team.
Virtual teams adhere to the same quality standards as traditional teams, but there are also subtle differences between the two.
Virtual team members often serve as a bridge for communication with their immediate physical group.
They have greater autonomy than traditional teams because their teams can meet across different time zones, which their local management cannot understand.
The presence of a true "invisible team" (Larson & LaFasto, 1989, p. 109) is also a distinctive feature of a virtual team.
The invisible team is the team to which every member is required to report. The invisible team sets the standard for every member.
