Although AI is a prominent theme in science fiction, it is an important branch of computer science concerned with building systems, dealing with behavior, learning, and acquiring intelligence in machines.
Research in AI involves creating machines that automate tasks requiring intelligent behavior.
Examples include control, planning, and scheduling, client identification and ability to answer questions, as well as handwriting, voice, and facial recognition.
Such things have become separate subjects focused on providing solutions to real-life problems.
AI systems are currently widely used in fields such as economics, medicine, engineering, and the military, including the development of numerous home computer and video game software applications.
A field trip is an integral part of any classroom. Often, a teacher would like to take her students to places where travel by bus is not an option.
Technology provides a solution through virtual field trips. Students can view rare museum specimens, tour aquaria, or appreciate beautiful artworks while sitting in their classrooms.
Participating in a virtual world field trip is an excellent way to reflect on this trip and contribute experiences to future classes.
For example, each year, students at Bennett School in Northern Kiro Line design a website for their journey to the state capital. Each year, the website is completely rebuilt from scratch, but the old version is kept online to serve as a scrapbook.
Blogs can also improve students' writing skills. Students begin their blogging with grammar and spelling mistakes, but eventually, the blog readership corrects these errors.
Since students are generally fond of criticism, the blog writer strives to improve his writing in order to avoid criticism.
Blogging also "compels students to become more aware of the world around them." The need to engage readers (audiences) encourages students to be alert and interesting (Toto, 2004).
Blogging is a tool that encourages collaboration and promotes students' learning skills beyond traditional school days.
Appropriate use of blogs can help students become more analytical and critical, enabling them to actively engage with online content, position themselves in relation to others' writings, and outline their own perspectives on specific issues (Owoc, 2002).
