The foundation of the modern city of Casa Blanca was laid by Berber fishermen in the 10th century BCE, and it was later used as a strategic port by the Phoenicians, Romans, and Mauri, who named it Anfa.
The Portuguese destroyed it and had it rebuilt under the name Casa Branca, only to abandon it after the 1755 earthquake.
The Sultan of Morocco rebuilt the city under the name Dar al-Baida, and the Spanish merchants, who established their trading settlements here, named it Casa Blanca.
Casablanca Port is one of the least preferred places for shopping in Morocco.
Near the old city of Medina, such places can easily be found where traditional Moroccan items such as tagines, clayware, leather goods, hookahs, and various types of toys are sold, but all these items are meant only for tourists.
Goma is a tourist city located in the far east of Rwanda, in the Democratic Republic of the Congo.
In 2002, Goma was destroyed by lava from the Nyiragongo volcano, which engulfed most of the city's roads, particularly those in the city center.
Although Goma is relatively safe, investigations are necessary to understand the ongoing fighting in North Kivu province, especially regarding any travel outside Goma.
This city serves as a base for tracking some elusive mountain gorillas in Africa, as well as for climbing the Nyiragongo volcano.
You can use a boda boda (motorcycle taxi) to travel around Goma. The normal (regional) fare for a short trip is 500 Congolese francs.
The name "Tumbuto," meaning inaccessible, was used as a metaphor for foreign, distant regions.
Today, Timbuktu is a poor region, although its reputation makes it a highly attractive tourist destination, and it has an airport.
In 1990, it was included in the list of endangered World Heritage sites due to the threat posed by desert sand.
One of the notable places to see during Henry Louis Gates' PBS Special on the African World was this.
This city is completely different from all other cities in the country, as it predominantly reflects an Arab temperament rather than an African one.
Kruger National Park (KNP) is located in northeastern South Africa, stretching from the border with Mozambique in the east to Zimbabwe in the north and the Limpopo River in the south.
This park covers an area of 19,500 square kilometers and is divided into 14 distinct ecological zones, each of which is conducive to a different type of wildlife.
It is one of the basic attractive places in South Africa, and it is considered one of the most important features of South African National Parks (SAN Parks).
Like all South African national parks, this park also has a daily fee for protection and entry.
It could also be beneficial for anyone looking to purchase a wildcard, which provides entry to selected parks in South Africa or all of South Africa's national parks.
The region of Hong Kong is named after the island of Hong Kong, and it is the place that many tourists consider as their center for tourism.
The rows of buildings that make up Hong Kong's skyline now form a glowing bar chart, clearly visible in the waters of Victoria Harbour.
To get the best view of Hong Kong, leave the island and go to the waterfront opposite Kowloon.
Hong Kong has a large number of urban constructions on land made usable, located along the northern coast of the island.
This is the place that the British imperialists turned into their own, and similarly, if you're looking for evidence of the region's colonial past, this is a good place to start.
Sundarbans is the largest coastal mangrove belt in the world, stretching 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coastal areas into the remote, underdeveloped regions of Bangladesh and India.
Sundarbans has been designated as a UNESCO World Heritage site. The portion of the forest located in the Indian region is known as Sundarbans National Park.
Forests are not merely mangrove swamps—they include some remaining strips of great forests that once covered the Gangetic plains.
Sundarbans covers an area of 3,850 km², of which one-third is water/mangrove swamp.
Since 1966, Sundarbans has been a wildlife sanctuary, and it is estimated that the area now has about 400 Bengal tigers and nearly 30,000 spotted deer.
Buses run throughout the day between bus stations located in different districts (across the river), although those specifically heading east and toward Jeker/Bumthang depart between 06:30 and 07:30.
Since buses running between districts are often full, it would be better to buy tickets a few days in advance.
In most districts, small Japanese coach buses are operated, which are comfortable and durable.
Shared taxis are faster and more comfortable for traveling to nearby destinations, such as Paro (No. 150) and Punakha (No. 200).
The Oyapock River Bridge is a cable-stayed bridge that connects the cities of Saint-Georges d'Oyapock in Brazil and French Guiana across the Oyapock River.
The height of both towers reaches 83 meters, and they are 378 meters long, with both lanes being 3.50 meters wide.
The vertical clearance under the bridge is 15 meters. Its construction was completed in August 2011, and it was not opened for traffic until March 2017.
It has been decided to fully implement it from September 2017, when the complete establishment of Brazilian customs checkpoints is expected.
The Guarani were the most important indigenous group, currently residing in Eastern Paraguay as semi-nomadic hunters, practicing agriculture as needed.
The Chaco region was home to other local tribal groups such as the Guaycuru and Payaguá, who lived by hunting, gathering, and fishing.
In the 16th century, Pragoe—formerly known as the "Giant Province of the Indies"—came into existence as a result of battles between the Spanish conquerors and the local indigenous communities.
The Spaniards initiated the era of feudalism, which lasted for three centuries.
After the founding of Asunción in 1537, Paraguay successfully maintained its indigenous character and identity.
Argentina is famous for having some of the best polo teams and players in the world.
The largest tournament of the year is held in December at the polo grounds located in Las Cañadas.
In other times of the year, one can also witness lower-level tournaments and matches here.
For information on news related to the tournament and where to buy tickets for polo matches, check the Association Argentina de Polo.
The official currency of the Falkland Islands is the Falkland pound (FKP), which is pegged in value to the British pound (GBP).
Currency exchange can be done at the island's only bank, located in front of the F.I. West Store in Stanley.
British pounds are generally accepted anywhere on the islands and also within Stanley credit cards, and US dollars are often accepted as well.
Credit cards from outer islands are likely not accepted, although British, European, and American currencies can be used; contact the owners in advance to confirm the acceptable payment methods.
It is almost impossible to exchange the currency of the Falkland Islands outside the islands, so exchange your money before leaving the islands.
Since Montevideo lies south of the equator, it experiences summer when the northern hemisphere has winter, and vice versa.
Montevideo lies in the subtropics; during the summer months, temperatures above 30 degrees Celsius are common.
Winter can be surprisingly cold: temperatures occasionally drop below the freezing point, but wind and humidity make the weather feel much colder than what the thermometer indicates.
There is no fixed time for the "wet" and "dry" seasons; the amount of rainfall remains fairly uniform throughout the year.
Many animals in Gou Park are accustomed to seeing humans, but wild animals remain wild and should neither be fed nor teased.
According to park officials, stay at least 100 yards/meters away from bears and lions, and at least 25 yards/meters away from other wild animals!
Although they may appear very polite on the surface, arna, bharah, musk ox, bear, and nearly every large animal can attack.
Every year, dozens of visitors get injured because they do not maintain a safe distance. These animals are large, wild, and potentially dangerous, so give them space.
Also keep in mind that foul-smelling food attracts bears and other wild animals. Therefore, avoid bringing or cooking smelly food and keep your camp clean.
Apia is the capital of Samoa. The city is located on the island of Upolu and has a population of around forty thousand (40,000).
